            <div xml:id="d819">

                <pb facs="1116" n="996" xml:id="pg996"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">211.18/37</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Chairman of the Senate Committee on Foreign
                        Relations</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Lodge</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1923-02-09"><hi rendition="#italic">February 9,
                        1923</hi></date>.</dateline></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">My Dear Mr. Secretary</hi>: The President’s Message
                    about the Costa Rican Treaty came in yesterday afternoon. I had the former vote
                    of ratification reconsidered and the treaty was again ratified, as were also the
                    notes in the form suggested in the Message, so that there has been no loss of
                    time. I read over your letter of transmittal and find that it was my stupidity
                    which made me overlook the fact that you wanted the notes confirmed as well as
                    the treaty. I ought to have observed it but it is an unusual proceeding and,
                    although I noted your allusion to Portugal, I quite failed to observe that one
                    point.</p>
                <p>Sincerely yours,</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">H. C. Lodge</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
